# zenpng scalar-vs-SIMD on aarch64 (NEON) — first ARM run of this bench
date:    2026-07-28
host:    Apple M4 Pro (12 core), macOS, aarch64
command: cargo bench --bench scalar_vs_simd --features _dev
build:   release, NO -C target-cpu=native
size:    large_4096x4096 (16 MP)

zenpng already had this bench and the `_dev` feature wiring
(archmage/testable_dispatch); it had simply never been recorded on ARM. On
aarch64 the `simd_512` rows dispatch to the NEON variants that magetypes
generates, so this is a NEON-vs-scalar comparison.

| predicate               | scalar     | SIMD (NEON) | speedup |
|-------------------------|------------|-------------|---------|
| fused_three_checks      | 19.068ms   |  2.483ms    |  7.68x  |
| bit_replication_be16    | 16.948ms   |  2.692ms    |  6.30x  |
| is_grayscale_rgb8       |  5.479ms   |  0.825ms    |  6.64x  |
| is_grayscale_rgba8      |  4.943ms   |  1.085ms    |  4.55x  |
| alpha_is_binary_rgba8   |  4.893ms   |  1.340ms    |  3.65x  |
| is_opaque_rgba8         |  99.6ns    |  146.1ns    |  see below |

The bench's own ship criterion is "beat scalar by >=10%". Every predicate that
does real work clears it by a wide margin — this is among the strongest NEON
payoffs measured across the zen crates, comparable to linear-srgb (3.3-6.6x)
and zenresize's linear path (4.0-4.3x).

`is_opaque_rgba8` is NOT a regression to act on: it is an early-exit predicate
that bails on the first non-opaque pixel, so at 16 MP it completes in ~100 ns
and the measurement is dominated by dispatch and timer overhead — zenbench
reports CV=46-51% on those two rows, i.e. noise. Reading a 0.68x "slowdown" out
of two 100ns samples with 50% variance would be exactly the kind of unverified
claim the neighbouring benchmark records warn about. If this path ever matters,
measure it with an input that has no early exit.

The `95% CI vs base` column shows absurd percentages (+2851853%) because
zenbench compares every row against the first row in the group, which is the
~100ns is_opaque baseline. Compare the mean column, not that one.
